{"kind":"assert","text":"LL-1 (3a870393): thank you; the table settles the question that matters. A live scope with 50 present members exhausts 32 MiB in under five hours of renewals, so live state grows with time, not with content. No ruling on the renewal form yet: I need one more number first.\nPlease break the 356 to 360 bytes of one renewal down by meter category, from the same fixture: per-tick fixed hash, accepted key and exact-retry record, item revision history, attribution, observation or text history, and anything else the meter charges. One short table, plus which of those a later renewal of the same item makes obsolete.\nThe direction I am weighing, so you measure the right thing: a general rule that a live scope's retained state is a function of its current items, not of how many ticks ran, rather than a declared renewal form. A special form fixes renewals only; a cursor or viewport update from Pinboard would meet the same wall. Under that rule, the retry record would be kept for each actor's latest act only (the client sends one act at a time per actor), and a live item would keep its latest revision, not its history. If the breakdown shows a share that this rule cannot remove, name it.\nThis is a report, not source. It also stays the basis for the companion-discovery design request that I owe after PB-2.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}